@subsection Expressions

One easy way to get started at programming is to examine some typical
fragments of a program in action.  Rust has no interactive
@newterm{read-eval-print loop}: a Rust program is compiled as a whole and
then run, so this book shows
what Scheme's interpreter transcript showed as runnable example programs,
each one printed at the point its book transcript appeared.  In each listing,
the printed results appear as comment lines beginning with @code{// =>}, and
the same values are asserted in the example's code, so every transcript in
this book is true by construction.  You type an @newterm{expression} into a
source file, the compiler checks it, and the running program displays the
result of its @newterm{evaluating} that expression.

One kind of primitive expression you might write is a number.  (More precisely,
the expression that you write consists of the numerals that represent the number
in base 10.)  If you present Rust with a number

@example rust
486
@end example

@noindent
the program will respond by printing@footnote{Throughout this book, when
we wish to emphasize the distinction between the expression written in the
source and the response printed by the running program, we will show the
latter as a @code{// =>} comment line.}

@example rust
println!("@{486@}");
// => 486
@end example

@noindent
Expressions representing numbers may be combined with an expression
representing a primitive operation (such as @code{+} or @code{*}) to form a
compound expression that represents the application of the operation to those
numbers.  For @w{example}:

@example rust
println!("@{@}", 137 + 349);
// => 486

println!("@{@}", 1000 - 334);
// => 666

println!("@{@}", 5 * 99);
// => 495

println!("@{@}", 10 / 5);
// => 2

println!("@{@}", 2.7 + 10.0);
// => 12.7
@end example

@noindent
Expressions such as these, formed by writing an operator between the
expressions that denote its arguments, are the closest Rust comes to the
book's @newterm{combinations}.  The vocabulary differs from Lisp's in one
visible way: Lisp writes every combination in @newterm{prefix notation},
with the operator leftmost, while Rust writes its primitive arithmetic
operators in the customary infix position (@code{137 + 349}, not
@code{(+ 137 349)}).  A function call is prefix-shaped the way a Lisp
combination is---the operator's name comes first,
@code{sum_of_squares(3, 4)}---but the operators proper are written between
their operands, and each function and operator takes a fixed number of
arguments, so the book's variadic forms such as @code{(+ 21 35 12 7)} become
@code{21 + 35 + 12 + 7}, one operator repeated.  What matters for this
chapter is what the two notations share: in both, the value of a compound
expression is obtained by applying the procedure specified by the
@newterm{operator} to the @newterm{arguments} that are the values of the
@newterm{operands}, and in both
the notation is unambiguous about which operator applies to which operands.

A further advantage the book claims for prefix notation is that it extends in
a straightforward way to allow combinations to be @i{nested}, that is, to have
combinations whose elements are themselves combinations.  Rust nests the same
way:

@example rust
println!("@{@}", (3 * 5) + (10 - 6));
// => 19
@end example

@noindent
There is no limit (in principle) to the depth of such nesting and to the
overall complexity of the expressions that the Rust compiler will accept and
evaluate.  It is we humans who get confused by still relatively simple
expressions such as

@example rust
3 * (2 * 4 + (3 + 5)) + (10 - 7 + 6)
@end example

@noindent
which the compiler will accept and the program will evaluate to be 57.  We
can help ourselves by writing such an expression in the form

@example rust
3 * (2 * 4
     + (3 + 5))
   + (10 - 7
      + 6)
@end example

@noindent
following a formatting convention known as @newterm{pretty-printing}, in which
each long expression is written so that the operands are aligned vertically.
The resulting indentations display clearly the structure of the
expression.@footnote{Rust systems provide a tool that automates this
formatting: @code{cargo fmt} lays every expression out in the community's
pretty-print convention, so no programmer in this book's examples formats an
expression by hand or argues about indentation.  Editors likewise highlight
the matching parenthesis or brace as you type.}

Even with complex expressions, the compiled program always operates in the
same basic cycle: it fetches the next instruction, evaluates it, and moves
on; there is no moment where an interpreter stops to print anything unless
the source asks it to.  Observe in particular that it is not necessary to
explicitly instruct the program to compute the value of an expression; the
value is what evaluation produces.@footnote{In Rust, as in Lisp, every
expression has a value, and @code{if}, @code{match}, and a block
@code{@{}...@code{@}} are themselves expressions that produce the value of
the branch or block taken.  The one deliberate exception is the statement:
an expression followed by a semicolon is evaluated for its effect, and its
value is discarded.}

@node	1.1.2, 1.1.3, 1.1.1, 1.1
@subsection Naming and the Environment

A critical aspect of a programming language is the means it provides for using
names to refer to computational objects.  We say that the name identifies a
@newterm{variable} whose @newterm{value} is the object.

In Rust, we name things with @code{let}.  Writing

@example rust
let size = 2;
@end example

@noindent
causes the program to associate the value 2 with the name
@code{size}.@footnote{A @code{let} binding in Rust is a compile-time
construct: the compiler checks every later use of the name against the type
of the value it names, and the running program simply uses the value.} Once
the name @code{size} has been associated with the number 2, we can refer to the
value 2 by name:

@example rust
println!("@{size@}");
// => 2

println!("@{@}", 5 * size);
// => 10
@end example

@noindent
Here are further examples of the use of @code{let}:

@example rust
let pi = 3.14159;
let radius = 10.0;

println!("@{@}", pi * radius * radius);
// => 314.159

let circumference = 2.0 * pi * radius;

println!("@{circumference@}");
// => 62.8318
@end example

@noindent
@code{Let} is our language's simplest means of abstraction, for it allows us
to use simple names to refer to the results of compound operations, such as the
@code{circumference} computed above.  In general, computational objects may
have very complex structures, and it would be extremely inconvenient to have to
remember and repeat their details each time we want to use them.  Indeed,
complex programs are constructed by building, step by step, computational
objects of increasing complexity.  Where the book's Lisp session builds this
way one interaction at a time, a Rust program grows the same way in the
source: each new name is written down once, checked by the compiler, and
thereafter available by name, which gives the incremental style a guarantee
the interactive session never had.

It should be clear that the possibility of associating values with names and
later retrieving them means that some sort of bookkeeping must keep track of
the name-object pairs.  This bookkeeping is called the
@newterm{environment} (more precisely the @newterm{global environment}, since
we will see later that a computation may involve a number of different
environments).  In Rust the environment of a program is its set of scopes:
each block, function, and module is a scope in which names are bound, and the
``global'' names of a program are the items of its crate, organized into
modules as described in @ref{0.9}.@footnote{@ref{Chapter 3} will show that this notion of
environment is essential, both for understanding how programs work and
for implementing interpreters.}

@node	1.1.3, 1.1.4, 1.1.2, 1.1
@subsection Evaluating Combinations

One of our goals in this chapter is to isolate issues about thinking
procedurally.  As a case in point, let us consider that, in evaluating
combinations, the language implementation is itself following a procedure.

@quotation
To evaluate a combination, do the following:

@enumerate 1

@item
Evaluate the subexpressions of the combination.

@item
Apply the procedure that is the value of the leftmost subexpression (the
operator) to the arguments that are the values of the other subexpressions (the
operands).

@end enumerate
@end quotation

@noindent
Even this simple rule illustrates some important points about processes in
general.  First, observe that the first step dictates that, to accomplish the
evaluation process for a combination, we must first perform the
evaluation process on each element of the combination.  Thus, the evaluation
rule is @newterm{recursive} in nature; that is, it includes, as one of its
steps, the need to invoke the rule itself.@footnote{It may seem strange that
the evaluation rule says, as part of the first step, that we should evaluate
the leftmost element of a combination, since at this point that can only be an
operator such as @code{+} or @code{*} representing a built-in primitive
procedure such as addition or multiplication.  We will see later that it is
useful to be able to work with combinations whose operators are themselves
compound expressions.}

Notice how succinctly the idea of recursion can be used to express what, in the
case of a deeply nested combination, would otherwise be viewed as a rather
complicated process.  For example, evaluating

@example rust
(2 + 4 * 6) * (3 + 5 + 7)
@end example

@noindent
requires that the evaluation rule be applied to four different combinations.
We can obtain a picture of this process by representing the combination in the
form of a tree, as shown in @ref{Figure 1.1}.  Each combination is represented
by a node with branches corresponding to the operator and the operands of the
combination stemming from it.  The terminal nodes (that is, nodes with no
branches stemming from them) represent either operators or numbers.  Viewing
evaluation in terms of the tree, we can imagine that the values of the operands
percolate upward, starting from the terminal nodes and then combining at higher
and higher levels.  In general, we shall see that recursion is a very powerful
technique for dealing with hierarchical, treelike objects.  In fact, the
``percolate values upward'' form of the evaluation rule is an example of a
general kind of process known as @newterm{tree accumulation}.

