@noindent
Next, observe that the repeated application of the first step brings us to the
point where we need to evaluate, not combinations, but primitive expressions
such as numerals, built-in operators, or other names.  We take care of the
primitive cases by stipulating that

@itemize @bullet

@item
the values of numerals are the numbers that they name,

@item
the values of built-in operators are the machine instruction sequences that
carry out the corresponding operations, and

@item
the values of other names are the objects associated with those names in the
environment.

@end itemize

@noindent
We may regard the second rule as a special case of the third one by stipulating
that names such as @code{+} and @code{*} are also part of what the
environment makes available, associated with the sequences of machine instructions that
are their ``values.''  The key point to notice is the role of the environment
in determining the meaning of the names in expressions.  It is meaningless to
speak of the value of an expression such as @code{x + 1} without specifying
any information about the environment
that would provide a meaning for the name @code{x}.  Rust settles this
question once and for all at compile time: every name's meaning is fixed by
the scope in which it is written, which is why @ref{Chapter 3} must
introduce environments explicitly when it builds interpreters that decide
such questions at run time.

Notice that the evaluation rule given above does not handle definitions.  For
instance, evaluating @code{let x = 3;} does not apply some operation to two
arguments, one of which is the value of the name @code{x} and the other of
which is 3, since the purpose of the @code{let} is precisely to associate
@code{x} with a value.  (That is, @code{let x = 3;} is not an expression.)

Such exceptions to the general evaluation rule are called @newterm{special forms}.  
@code{Let}, along with the function definition @code{fn} we are about to
meet, is an example of a special form; we will meet others shortly.  Each special form has its own
evaluation rule. The various kinds of expressions (each with its associated
evaluation rule) constitute the syntax of the programming language.  Rust's
syntax is much larger than Lisp's; that is, the evaluation rule for
expressions is described by a general rule together with specialized rules
for a larger set of special forms and declarations, most of which
(@code{match}, @code{if}, @code{loop}, and their companions) the chapter 0
primer already introduced.@footnote{Special syntactic forms that are simply convenient
alternative surface structures for things that can be written in more uniform
ways are sometimes called @newterm{syntactic sugar}, to use a phrase coined by
Peter Landin.  In comparison with Lisp, whose surface syntax is nearly
nonexistent, Rust carries a good deal of syntactic sugar---method-call
syntax, range expressions, the @code{?} operator---each of which the
compiler expands to a more uniform underlying form.  In the words of Alan
Perlis, ``Syntactic sugar causes cancer of the semicolon.''; Rust manages the
dose by making every expansion checkable and by refusing to evaluate anything
the compiler has not already typed.}

@node	1.1.4, 1.1.5, 1.1.3, 1.1
@subsection Compound Procedures

We have identified in Rust some of the elements that must appear in any
powerful programming language:

@itemize @bullet

@item
Numbers and arithmetic operations are primitive data and procedures.

@item
Nesting of combinations provides a means of combining operations.

@item
Definitions that associate names with values provide a limited means of
abstraction.

@end itemize

@noindent
Now we will learn about @newterm{procedure definitions}, a much more powerful
abstraction technique by which a compound operation can be given a name and
then referred to as a unit.

We begin by examining how to express the idea of ``squaring.''  We might say,
``To square something, multiply it by itself.''  This is expressed in our
language as

@example rust
fn square(x: i64) -> i64 @{
    x * x
@}
@end example

@noindent
We can understand this in the following way:

@example
fn square(x: i64) -> i64 @{    x * x    @}
    |      |         |          |
 To square something, multiply it by itself.
@end example

@noindent
We have here a @newterm{compound procedure}, which has been given the name
@code{square}.  The procedure represents the operation of multiplying something
by itself.  The thing to be multiplied is given a local name, @code{x}, which
plays the same role that a pronoun plays in natural language.  Evaluating the
definition creates this compound procedure and associates it with the name
@code{square}.@footnote{Observe that there are two different operations being
combined here: we are creating the procedure, and we are giving it the name
@code{square}.  It is possible, indeed important, to be able to separate these
two notions---to create procedures without naming them, and to give names to
procedures that have already been created.  We will see how to do this in
@ref{1.3.2}.}

The general form of a procedure definition is

@example rust
fn @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩}(@code{⟨}@var{parameters}@code{⟩}: @code{⟨}@var{parameter types}@code{⟩}) -> @code{⟨}@var{return type}@code{⟩} @{
    @code{⟨}@var{body}@code{⟩}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩} is a symbol to be associated with the procedure definition in
the environment.@footnote{Throughout this book, we will describe the general
syntax of expressions by using italic symbols delimited by angle
brackets---e.g., @code{⟨}@var{name}@code{⟩}---to denote the ``slots'' in the expression to be
filled in when such an expression is actually used.} The @code{⟨}@var{parameters}@code{⟩}
are the names used within the body of the procedure to refer to
the corresponding arguments of the procedure.  The @code{⟨}@var{body}@code{⟩} is an
expression that will yield the value of the procedure application when the
formal parameters are replaced by the actual arguments to which the procedure
is applied.@footnote{More generally, the body of the procedure can be a
sequence of statements ending in an expression.  In this case, the program
evaluates each statement in the sequence in turn and returns the value of the final
expression as the value of the procedure application.}  Rust adds one
requirement Lisp does not have: the compiler must also be told the types of
the parameters and of the result, so that every application can be
type-checked before the program ever runs.

Having defined @code{square}, we can now use it:

@example rust
println!("@{@}", square(21));
// => 441

println!("@{@}", square(2 + 5));
// => 49

println!("@{@}", square(square(3)));
// => 81
@end example

@noindent
We can also use @code{square} as a building block in defining other procedures.
For example, @math{x^2 + y^2} can be expressed as

@example rust
square(x) + square(y)
@end example

@noindent
We can easily define a procedure @code{sum_of_squares} that, given any two
numbers as arguments, produces the sum of their squares:

@example rust
fn sum_of_squares(x: i64, y: i64) -> i64 @{
    square(x) + square(y)
@}

println!("@{@}", sum_of_squares(3, 4));
// => 25
@end example

@noindent
Now we can use @code{sum_of_squares} as a building block in constructing
further procedures:

@example rust
fn f(a: i64) -> i64 @{
    sum_of_squares(a + 1, a * 2)
@}

println!("@{@}", f(5));
// => 136
@end example

@noindent
Compound procedures are used in exactly the same way as primitive procedures.
Indeed, one could not tell by looking at the uses of
@code{sum_of_squares} given above whether @code{square} was built into the
standard library or defined here as a compound procedure.

@node	1.1.5, 1.1.6, 1.1.4, 1.1
@subsection The Substitution Model for Procedure Application

To evaluate a combination whose operator names a compound procedure, the
language follows much the same process as for combinations whose operators
name primitive procedures, which we described in @ref{1.1.3}.  That is,
the program evaluates the elements of the combination and applies the
procedure (which is the value of the operator of the combination) to the
arguments (which are the values of the operands of the combination).

We can assume that the mechanism for applying primitive procedures to arguments
is built into the implementation.  For compound procedures, the application
process is as follows:

@quotation
To apply a compound procedure to arguments, evaluate the body of the procedure
with each formal parameter replaced by the corresponding argument.
@end quotation

@noindent
To illustrate this process, let's evaluate the combination

@example rust
f(5)
@end example

@noindent
where @code{f} is the procedure defined in @ref{1.1.4}.  We begin by
retrieving the body of @code{f}:

@example rust
sum_of_squares(a + 1, a * 2)
@end example

@noindent
Then we replace the formal parameter @code{a} by the argument 5:

@example rust
sum_of_squares(5 + 1, 5 * 2)
@end example

@noindent
Thus the problem reduces to the evaluation of a combination with two operands
and an operator @code{sum_of_squares}.  Evaluating this combination involves
three subproblems.  We must evaluate the operator to get the procedure to be
applied, and we must evaluate the operands to get the arguments.  Now @code{5
+ 1} produces 6 and @code{5 * 2} produces 10, so we must apply the
@code{sum_of_squares} procedure to 6 and 10.  These values are substituted for
the formal parameters @code{x} and @code{y} in the body of
@code{sum_of_squares}, reducing the expression to

@example rust
square(6) + square(10)
@end example

@noindent
If we use the definition of @code{square}, this reduces to

@example rust
6 * 6 + 10 * 10
@end example

@noindent
which reduces by multiplication to

@example rust
36 + 100
@end example

@noindent
and finally to

@example rust
136
@end example

@noindent
The process we have just described is called the @newterm{substitution model}
for procedure application.  It can be taken as a model that determines the
``meaning'' of procedure application, insofar as the procedures in this chapter
are concerned.  However, there are two points that should be stressed:

@itemize @bullet

@item
The purpose of the substitution is to help us think about procedure
application, not to provide a description of how the implementation really works.
The Rust compiler does not evaluate procedure applications by manipulating the
text of a procedure to substitute values for the formal parameters.  In
practice, the ``substitution'' is accomplished by the compiled code passing
the arguments in registers or on the stack, and by a frame that carries the
procedure's local names.  We will discuss this more fully in @ref{Chapter 3} and
@ref{Chapter 4} when we examine the implementation of an interpreter in detail.

@item
Over the course of this book, we will present a sequence of increasingly
elaborate models of how programs work, culminating with a complete
implementation of an interpreter and compiler in @ref{Chapter 5}.  The
substitution model is only the first of these models---a way to get started
thinking formally about the evaluation process.  In general, when modeling
phenomena in science and engineering, we begin with simplified, incomplete
models.  As we examine things in greater detail, these simple models become
inadequate and must be replaced by more refined models.  The substitution model
is no exception.  In particular, when we address in @ref{Chapter 3} the use of
procedures with ``mutable data,'' we will see that the substitution model
breaks down and must be replaced by a more complicated model of procedure
application.@footnote{Despite the simplicity of the substitution idea, it turns
out to be surprisingly complicated to give a rigorous mathematical definition
of the substitution process.  The problem arises from the possibility of
confusion between the names used for the formal parameters of a procedure and
the (possibly identical) names used in the expressions to which the procedure
may be applied.  Indeed, there is a long history of erroneous definitions of
@newterm{substitution} in the literature of logic and programming semantics.
See @ref{Stoy 1977} for a careful discussion of substitution.}

@end itemize

@subsubheading Applicative order versus normal order

According to the description of evaluation given in @ref{1.1.3}, the
implementation first evaluates the operator and operands and then applies the
resulting procedure to the resulting arguments.  This is not the only way to
perform evaluation.  An alternative evaluation model would not evaluate the
operands until their values were needed.  Instead it would first substitute
operand expressions for parameters until it obtained an expression involving
only primitive operators, and would then perform the evaluation.  If we used
this method, the evaluation of @code{f(5)} would proceed according to the
sequence of expansions

@example rust
sum_of_squares(5 + 1, 5 * 2)

square(5 + 1) + square(5 * 2)

(5 + 1) * (5 + 1) + (5 * 2) * (5 * 2)
@end example

@noindent
followed by the reductions

@example rust
6 * 6 + 10 * 10

36 + 100

136
@end example

@noindent
This gives the same answer as our previous evaluation model, but the process is
different.  In particular, the evaluations of @code{5 + 1} and @code{5 * 2}
are each performed twice here, corresponding to the reduction of the expression
@code{x * x} with @code{x} replaced respectively by @code{5 + 1} and
@code{5 * 2}.

This alternative ``fully expand and then reduce'' evaluation method is known as
@newterm{normal-order evaluation}, in contrast to the ``evaluate the arguments
and then apply'' method that Rust actually uses, which is called
@newterm{applicative-order evaluation}.  It can be shown that, for procedure
applications that can be modeled using substitution (including all the
procedures in the first two chapters of this book) and that yield legitimate
values, normal-order and applicative-order evaluation produce the same value.
(See @ref{Exercise 1.5} for an instance of an ``illegitimate'' value where
normal-order and applicative-order evaluation do not give the same result.)

Rust uses applicative-order evaluation, partly because of the additional
efficiency obtained from avoiding multiple evaluations of expressions such as
those illustrated with @code{5 + 1} and @code{5 * 2} above and, more
significantly, because normal-order evaluation becomes much more complicated to deal with when we move beyond procedures that can be modeled by
substitution.  On the other hand, normal-order evaluation can be an extremely
valuable tool, and we will investigate some of its implications in @ref{Chapter
3} and @ref{Chapter 4}.@footnote{In @ref{Chapter 3} we will introduce
@newterm{stream processing}, which is a way of handling apparently ``infinite''
data structures by incorporating a limited form of normal-order evaluation; in
Rust, the iterator adapters of @ref{0.5} are the same tool under another
name.  In @ref{4.2} we will modify the interpreter of @ref{Chapter 4} to
evaluate the book's Scheme subset with normal-order delay, and the Rust
closures of @ref{0.6} are the ordinary-language stand-in for a thunk.}

@node	1.1.6, 1.1.7, 1.1.5, 1.1
@subsection Conditional Expressions and Predicates

The expressive power of the class of procedures that we can define at this
point is very limited, because we have no way to make tests and to perform
different operations depending on the result of a test.  For instance, we
cannot define a procedure that computes the absolute value of a number by
testing whether the number is positive, negative, or zero and taking different
actions in the different cases according to the rule
@displaymath
\left|{x}\right| \; = \;
  \cases{	 	 
       x & \hbox{if } $x > 0$, \cr
       0 & \hbox{if } $x  =  0$, \cr
      -x & \hbox{if } $x < 0$. 
    }
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
      /
      |   x  if x > 0
|x| = <   0  if x = 0
      |  -x  if x < 0
      \
@end example

@end ifinfo
This construct is called a @newterm{case analysis}, and Rust notates such a
case analysis with the @code{match} expression, which it uses as follows:

@example rust
fn abs(x: i64) -> i64 @{
    match x @{
        y if y > 0 => y,
        0 => 0,
        _ => -x,
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
The general form of a conditional expression is

@example rust
match @code{⟨}@var{subject}@code{⟩} @{
    @code{⟨}@var{pattern₁}@code{⟩} @code{⟨}@var{guard₁}@code{⟩} => @code{⟨}@var{e₁}@code{⟩},
    @code{⟨}@var{pattern₂}@code{⟩} @code{⟨}@var{guard₂}@code{⟩} => @code{⟨}@var{e₂}@code{⟩},
    @r{…}
    _ => @code{⟨}@var{eₙ}@code{⟩},
@}
@end example

@noindent
consisting of the @code{match} keyword, a subject expression, and a sequence
of @newterm{clauses}, one per line, each of the form

@example rust
@code{⟨}@var{pattern}@code{⟩} => @code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩},
@end example

@noindent
The clauses are tried in order; the first whose pattern matches the subject
(and whose optional @code{if} @newterm{guard} holds) is taken.  The final
@code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} of the taken clause is the value of the whole
@code{match}.  Whether a clause applies is decided by expressions whose value
is interpreted as either true or false, and each such decision expression is
a @newterm{predicate}---that is, an expression whose value is interpreted as
either true or false.@footnote{Rust is stricter about this than Scheme: the
type @code{bool} has exactly two values, @code{true} and @code{false}, and a
condition or guard must be a @code{bool}.  Scheme accepts any value and
treats every value but @code{#f} as true; Rust has no such truthiness, so a
condition that would have relied on it must be written out, such as
@code{x != 0} instead of a bare @code{x}.}

Conditional expressions are evaluated as follows.  The patterns and guards of
the clauses are examined in order.  If a clause's guard is false, the next
clause is examined.  This process continues until a clause is found whose
pattern matches and whose guard is true, in which case the value of the
corresponding @newterm{consequent expression}
@code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} of the clause becomes the value of the conditional expression.
Unlike Lisp's @code{cond}, a Rust @code{match} is never left without a
taken clause: the compiler rejects the program unless the clauses
collectively cover every possible subject, with @code{_} available for the
catch-all.

The word @newterm{predicate} is used for functions that return @code{bool},
as well as for expressions that evaluate to @code{bool}.  The absolute-value
procedure @code{abs} makes use of the primitive comparisons @code{>},
@code{<}, and @code{==}.@footnote{@code{Abs} also uses the ``minus'' operator
@code{-}, which, when written before its operand, as in @code{-x}, indicates
negation.} These take two numbers as arguments and test whether the first
number is, respectively, greater than, less than, or equal to the second
number, returning @code{true} or @code{false} accordingly.

Another way to write the absolute-value procedure is

@example rust
fn abs(x: i64) -> i64 @{
    match x @{
        y if y < 0 => -y,
        _ => x,
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
which could be expressed in English as ``If @math{x} is less than zero return
@math{{-x}}; otherwise return @math{x}.''  The @code{_} pattern plays the role
of the book's @code{else}: it matches whatever the earlier clauses did not.
This causes the @code{match} to return as its value the value of the corresponding
@code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} whenever all previous clauses have been bypassed.

Here is yet another way to write the absolute-value procedure:

@example rust
fn abs(x: i64) -> i64 @{
    if x < 0 @{
        -x
    @} else @{
        x
    @}
@}
@end example

@noindent
This uses the @code{if} expression, a restricted type of conditional that can
be used when there are precisely two cases in the case analysis.  The general
form of an @code{if} expression is

@example rust
if @code{⟨}@var{predicate}@code{⟩} @{
    @code{⟨}@var{consequent}@code{⟩}
@} else @{
    @code{⟨}@var{alternative}@code{⟩}
@}
@end example

@noindent
To evaluate an @code{if} expression, the program starts by evaluating the
@code{⟨}@var{predicate}@code{⟩} part of the expression.  If the @code{⟨}@var{predicate}@code{⟩} evaluates
to @code{true}, it then evaluates the @code{⟨}@var{consequent}@code{⟩} and
returns its value.  Otherwise it evaluates the @code{⟨}@var{alternative}@code{⟩} and returns
its value.@footnote{A @code{match} clause's @code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} and an
@code{if} arm are both blocks or single expressions: a block may hold a
sequence of statements, and the value of the block is the value of its final
expression, which is what the conditional yields.  The two arms of an
@code{if} must produce values of the same type, which the compiler checks.}

In addition to primitive comparisons such as @code{<}, @code{==}, and @code{>},
there are logical composition operations, which enable us to construct compound
predicates.  The three most frequently used are these:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{@code{⟨}@var{e₁}@code{⟩} && @r{…} && @code{⟨}@var{eₙ}@code{⟩}}

The program evaluates the expressions @code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} one at a time, in
left-to-right order.  If any @code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} evaluates to @code{false}, the value of the
whole expression is @code{false}, and the rest of the @code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩}'s are not
evaluated.  If all evaluate to @code{true}, the value is the value of the last one.

@item
@code{@code{⟨}@var{e₁}@code{⟩} || @r{…} || @code{⟨}@var{eₙ}@code{⟩}}

The program evaluates the expressions @code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} one at a time, in
left-to-right order.  If any @code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} evaluates to @code{true}, that value is
returned as the value of the whole expression, and the rest of the
@code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩}'s are not evaluated.  If all evaluate to @code{false}, the value
is @code{false}.

@item
@code{!@code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩}}

The value of a @code{!} expression is @code{true} when the expression
@code{⟨}@var{e}@code{⟩} evaluates to @code{false}, and @code{false} otherwise.

@end itemize

@noindent
Notice that @code{&&} and @code{||} are not ordinary calls: the right operand
is not necessarily evaluated at all, which no ordinary procedure could
arrange with eager arguments.  @code{!} is an ordinary operator.

As an example of how these are used, the condition that a number @math{x} be in
the range @math{5 < x < 10} may be expressed as

@example rust
x > 5 && x < 10
@end example

@noindent
As another example, we can define a predicate to test whether one number is
greater than or equal to another as

@example rust
fn geq(x: i64, y: i64) -> bool @{
    x > y || x == y
@}
@end example

@noindent
or alternatively as

@example rust
fn geq(x: i64, y: i64) -> bool @{
    !(x < y)
@}
@end example

@noindent
(The runnable listing of the case-analysis forms and the logical operators in
this subsection is @code{examples/sec_1_1_abs.rs}; the listings of the first
three subsections are @code{examples/sec_1_1_expressions.rs},
@code{examples/sec_1_1_naming.rs}, and @code{examples/sec_1_1_square.rs},
which also carries the substitution walk of @code{f(5)}.)

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 1.1}Exercise 1.1:} Below is a sequence of expressions.
What is the result produced by the program for each expression?
Assume that the sequence is to be evaluated in the order in which it is
presented.

@example rust
